{"task_id": "Mbpp/232", "solution": "def larg_nnum(list_of_numbers, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a list containing the n largest numeric items from the input list.\n    Includes input validation and edge case handling as specified in the design.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _validate_inputs(list_of_numbers_arg, n_arg):\n        \"\"\"Validates input types and numeric elements, raising TypeError on invalid input.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(list_of_numbers_arg, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"First argument must be a list.\")\n        if not isinstance(n_arg, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Second argument 'n' must be an integer.\")\n        for item in list_of_numbers_arg:\n            if not isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n                raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be numeric (int or float).\")\n\n    def _select_n_largest(list_of_numbers_arg, n_arg):\n        \"\"\"Implements the core logic to select n largest items, handling edge cases for n.\"\"\"\n        if n_arg <= 0:\n            return []\n        \n        if n_arg >= len(list_of_numbers_arg):\n            # Return all items, preserving multiplicity and original order (though order is non-significant)\n            return list(list_of_numbers_arg)\n        \n        # Sort the list in descending order and return the first n elements\n        # Using sorted() creates a new list, leaving the original unchanged.\n        sorted_list = sorted(list_of_numbers_arg, reverse=True)\n        return sorted_list[:n_arg]\n\n    _validate_inputs(list_of_numbers, n)\n    return _select_n_largest(list_of_numbers, n)"}
